    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d486">

                <pb facs="0905" n="801" sameAs="frus:frus1943CairoTehran/pg801"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Leahy Papers</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The President’s Chief of Staff</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Leahy</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        President</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d486-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Cairo</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-12-05">5 December 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Memorandum for the
                    President</hi>:</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Recognition of Rome as an Open City.</p>
                <p>The question of declaring Rome an open city has again been discussed by the Joint
                    U. S. Chiefs of Staff with the British Chiefs of Staff. The British Chiefs of
                    Staff are still of the opinion that, from a military point of view, such action
                    is undesirable.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William D. Leahy</hi></signed><lb/><hi
                        rendition="#italic">Admiral, U. S. Navy Chief of Staff to the Commander in
                        Chief of the Army and Navy</hi></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d486-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d486-f1-content">
                <p>Hull’s letter of November 21, 1943, to Roosevelt (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg266">p. 266</ref>) was referred to Leahy earlier
                    in the day on December 5, 1943, with a memorandum from Brown reading: “The
                    President requests that you discuss this matter with the British.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
